/-! # Dimension of intersections in affine space

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0B2Q, `varieties-lemma-intersection-in-affine-space`,
and its immediate consequence for a nonempty intersection
(blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-closed-intersection-dimension).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Every component of the intersection of equidimensional closed subsets of affine
`n`-space has dimension at least `a + b - n`. A nonempty intersection satisfies the
same bound. The inequalities are written additively in extended dimensions.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0B2Q, `varieties-lemma-intersection-in-affine-space`,
and its immediate nonempty-intersection consequence.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-closed-intersection-dimension)

Proof:
1. Put `R = k[x_1, ..., x_n]`. By `def-sbc-affine-space` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-spec-irreducible-closed-subsets-primes`, write `A = V(I)` and
   `B = V(J)`. Give these the closed subscheme structures `X = Spec(R/I)` and
   `Y = Spec(R/J)` using `lem-weil-pbc-spec-quotient-closed-immersion` and
   `def-weil-closed-immersion`. Primes and basic opens of a quotient identify their
   underlying spaces with the prescribed subspaces. Thus they are nonempty finite-type
   `k`-schemes equidimensional of dimensions `a` and `b`.
2. By `lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra`, their product is `T = Spec S`,
   where `S = (R/I) tensor_k (R/J)`, generated by the two lists `u_i` and `v_i`.
   Let `H = (u_i-v_i)` and `D = S/H`. Sending both generators to `x_i` gives
   `D = R/(I+J)`; the inverse sends `x_i` to the common class of `u_i` and `v_i`.
   Both composites fix the generators. The quotient closed immersion
   (`lem-weil-pbc-spec-quotient-closed-immersion`) identifies `Z = Spec D` with
   `A inter B`. All these rings are finite type over `k`; `S` is Noetherian by
   `lem-wf-affine-noetherian-components`.
3. The structure map of `X` is flat by `lem-weil-pbc-module-over-field-flat` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-affine-criterion`. Hence `T -> Y` is flat by
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-base-change`. It and `T -> Spec k` are finite type
   by `lem-sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change`.
4. At `z` of `T`, with images `x` and `y`, apply
   `lem-sc-fibre-dimension-after-base-change` and
   `lem-sbc-flat-composite-fibre-dimension-at-point`. The fibres over `Spec k`
   identify with the original schemes, giving `dim_z T = dim_x X + dim_y Y`.
   By `lem-sbc-equidimensional-open-local-dimension` on the whole opens these
   dimensions are `a` and `b`. Thus the local topological dimension of `T` is `a+b`.
5. By `lem-weil-pbc-spec-irreducible-components-minimal-primes`, a component `C`
   of `Z` is `V_D(q)` for a minimal prime `q`. Its inverse image `P` in `S` is
   the generic point of the image of `C` in `T` by
   `lem-weil-pbc-spec-irreducible-closed-subsets-primes`. The bound
   `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-height-quotient-bound` gives `height P <= height q + n`.
   Minimality gives `height q = 0`, so `height P <= n`, even if some differences
   repeat or vanish.
6. By `lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-localization-dimension-chains-below-prime`, the stalk of `T` at
   `P` has dimension `height P`. By `lem-kun-dimension-formula` and step 4,
   `a+b = height P + trdeg_k kappa(P)`. By
   `lem-sc-closed-irreducible-dimension-trdeg`, the last term is `dim C`.
   Therefore `a+b <= n + dim C`. The homeomorphism in step 2 transports this
   inequality to each component of `A inter B`.
7. If the intersection is nonempty its dimension is finite and at most `a`, by
   inclusion of closed irreducible chains. By `def-kun-topological-krull-dimension`,
   a chain attaining that dimension exists. Its last member must be a component:
   a larger irreducible closed subset would extend the chain. That component has
   the dimension of the intersection, so step 6 proves the second inequality.
   An empty intersection has no components, and its second conclusion is conditional.

The natural parameters encode nonnegative integers. For a nonempty space of
nonnegative dimension, `a+b <= dim C+n` is equivalent to `dim C >= a+b-n`
with integer subtraction, including when `a+b < n`. -/
theorem AffineSpace.closed_intersection_dimension (k : Type u) [Field k] (n : ℕ)
    (A B : Set (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin n)) (Spec (.of k))))
    (hAClosed : IsClosed A) (hBClosed : IsClosed B)
    (hANonempty : A.Nonempty) (hBNonempty : B.Nonempty)
    (a b : ℕ) (hADim : topologicalKrullDim A = (a : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (hBDim : topologicalKrullDim B = (b : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (hAEquidim : ∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents ↥A,
      topologicalKrullDim C = (a : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (hBEquidim : ∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents ↥B,
      topologicalKrullDim C = (b : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    (∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents ↥(A ∩ B),
      ((a + b : ℕ) : WithBot ℕ∞) ≤ topologicalKrullDim C + (n : WithBot ℕ∞)) ∧
    ((A ∩ B).Nonempty →
      ((a + b : ℕ) : WithBot ℕ∞) ≤ topologicalKrullDim ↥(A ∩ B) + (n : WithBot ℕ∞)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
